span.span:
- 3×1=3≡3(mod7)3\times 1=3\equiv 3\pmod{7}
- 3×2=6≡6(mod7)3\times 2=6\equiv 6\pmod{7}
- 3×3=9≡2(mod7)3\times 3=9\equiv 2\pmod{7}
- 3×4=12≡5(mod7)3\times 4=12\equiv 5\pmod{7}
- 3×5=15≡1(mod7)3\times 5=15\equiv 1\pmod{7}

n=1: 3∗1=3≡3≠2(mod7)3*1=3\equiv 3\neq 2\pmod{7}
n=2: 3∗2=6≡6≠2(mod7)3*2=6\equiv 6\neq 2\pmod{7}
n=3: 3∗3=9≡2(mod7)3*3=9\equiv 2\pmod{7}. Yes! So, n=3 is the smallest positive integer.
